\section{Analytical Observability, Gauge-Aware Live Stability, and Local ISS}
\label{app:iss-stability}

This section establishes local stability of the normal Live recursion without
requiring true-heading convergence, simultaneous vector updates, four
consecutive integral pseudo-updates, or a finite accelerometer-bias correlation
time in every admissible case.  The proof has two full-heading routes.  A
\emph{full-PE route} uses a windowed augmented observability Gramian for
attitude, gyro bias, and accelerometer bias and therefore permits the
accelerometer-bias random-walk limit.  An \emph{OU-fallback route} requires less
measurement excitation and instead uses intrinsic contraction of a finite-time-
constant residual accelerometer bias.  A separate gravity-only theorem gives
stability modulo the unobservable yaw gauge when no absolute heading reference
is available.

\subsection{Adopted horizontal gauge and error objective}
\label{sec:iss-heading-gauge}

A learned magnetic reference need not coincide exactly with true north.  A
consistent heading gauge is therefore a rotation of the complete horizontal
world frame, not of attitude alone.  Let
\begin{equation}
 \mat Q_\psi(t):=\mat R_z\!\left(b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}(t)\right)
 \label{eq:iss-gauge-rotation}
\end{equation}
and define the gauge-referenced physical trajectory by
\begin{align}
 \mat R_g(t)&=\mat Q_\psi(t)\mat R_{\rm true}(t),
 \label{eq:iss-gauge-reference}\\
 \vct v_g&=\mat Q_\psi\vct v_{\rm true},&
 \vct p_g&=\mat Q_\psi\vct p_{\rm true},\\
 \vct S_g&=\mat Q_\psi\vct S_{\rm true},&
 \vct a_{w,g}&=\mat Q_\psi\vct a_{w,\rm true}.
 \label{eq:iss-gauge-world-vectors}
\end{align}
Body-frame sensor biases are not rotated by this action.  Because
$\mat Q_\psi\vct g=\vct g$, the accelerometer model is invariant under the
simultaneous transformation of attitude and world acceleration.  The
$v\to p\to S$ chain, the isotropic OU drift operator, and the $S=0$
pseudo-measurement are likewise equivariant.  Thus a constant yaw-reference
offset defines another representation of the same horizontal physical motion.
Any non-yaw magnetic-model error (for example residual dip, scale, soft-iron, or
misalignment error) remains a bounded model disturbance rather than part of
this symmetry.

All world-frame state errors below are measured relative to the trajectory in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-gauge-world-vectors}; the local attitude error is measured
relative to $\mat R_g$.  Locally decompose
\begin{equation}
 \delta\vct\theta
 =\delta\vct\theta_\perp+\delta\psi_{\rm int}\vct d,
 \qquad
 \vct d^\top\delta\vct\theta_\perp=0,
 \label{eq:iss-tilt-yaw-split}
\end{equation}
where $\vct d$ is world down.  The true heading error is therefore
\begin{equation}
 \delta\psi_{\rm true}
 =\delta\psi_{\rm int}+b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}.
 \label{eq:iss-true-yaw-error}
\end{equation}
A constant $b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}$ changes the adopted horizontal gauge but does
not create a nonzero estimator error relative to that gauge.  If the gauge
varies slowly, $\dot{\mat Q}_\psi$ contributes bounded forcing to attitude and
to the gauge-referenced world-vector dynamics; on a compact physical-motion
family this forcing is bounded by $|\dot b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}|$ times the
corresponding world-vector magnitudes.  Accordingly, disturbance-free
convergence below means convergence to the adopted horizontal gauge; it does not
claim removal of residual magnetic calibration or reference bias.

\subsection{Exogenous scheduling and bounded Live family}
\label{app:iss-tuner}

The adaptive wave variables are exogenous signals,
\begin{equation}
 \vartheta_k=\bigl(\tau_k,\sigma_{aw,k},r_{S,k}\bigr)
 \in\mathcal F_{k-1},
 \label{eq:iss-schedule}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal F_k$ contains measurements through sample $k$.  The wave-period
and variance estimators read raw IMU data and the measurement-only proxy, never
OU--III state or covariance.  Thus the deterministic proof needs only a bounded
exogenous schedule, while the one-sample staging gives predictability for the
stochastic formulation.

Assume a Live interval on which
\begin{equation}
 0<h_{\min}\le h_k\le h_{\max}<\infty,
 \qquad
 0<\underline\tau\le\tau_k\le\overline\tau<\infty,
 \label{eq:iss-step-tau-bounds}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
 0\le\sigma_{aw,k}\le\overline\sigma<\infty,
 \qquad
 0<\underline r_S\le r_{S,k}\le\overline r_S<\infty.
 \label{eq:iss-tuning-bounds}
\end{equation}
No positive lower bound on $\sigma_{aw}$ is imposed merely for observability.
For the OU-fallback route only, assume
\begin{equation}
 0<\underline\tau_b\le\tau_b\le\overline\tau_b<\infty.
 \label{eq:iss-bias-tau-bounds}
\end{equation}
The full-PE route also admits $\tau_b=\infty$.

Measurement covariances satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \underline r\mat I
 \preceq \mat R_{a,k},\mat R_{m,k},\mat R_{S,k}
 \preceq \overline r\mat I,
 \qquad 0<\underline r\le\overline r<\infty.
 \label{eq:iss-R-bounds}
\end{equation}
Accepted vector rows obey compact magnitude and rate bounds
\begin{equation}
 f_0\le\|\widehat{\vct f}_b\|\le f_1,
 \qquad
 m_0\le\|\widehat{\vct m}_b\|\le m_1,
 \label{eq:iss-vector-magnitude-bounds}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
 \|\widehat{\vct\omega}_k\|\le\overline\omega<\infty.
 \label{eq:iss-vector-geometry}
\end{equation}
No pointwise accelerometer--magnetometer noncollinearity and no
$\overline\omega\Delta<\pi/2$ condition are assumed.  Heading/tilt information
is required through the windowed Gramian below instead.

No hard reset occurs inside an analyzed Live window.

\subsection{Four usable $S$ updates are sufficient}

For one translational axis,
\begin{equation}
 \dot v=a,\qquad
 \dot p=v,\qquad
 \dot S=p,\qquad
 \dot a=-\lambda(t)a,
 \qquad \lambda(t)=1/\tau(t),
 \label{eq:iss-scalar-chain}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 E(t)=\exp\!\left[-\int_0^t\lambda(s)\,ds\right],
 \qquad
 A_3(t)=\frac12\int_0^t(t-s)^2E(s)\,ds.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
 S(t)=S_0+t p_0+\frac{t^2}{2}v_0+A_3(t)a_0.
\end{equation}

Each translational proof window need only contain four \emph{usable} $S$
pseudo-updates, not four consecutive scheduler firings.  After shifting the
first time to zero, assume
\begin{equation}
 0=t_0<t_1<t_2<t_3\le T_{S,W},
 \qquad
 \min_{0\le i<j\le3}(t_j-t_i)\ge\Delta_{S,-}>0.
 \label{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds}
\end{equation}
Skipped or deferred pseudo-updates are therefore allowed provided another four
usable updates satisfying this bounded-window condition remain.

At those times,
\begin{equation}
 \mat O_S=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 t_0^2/2&t_0&1&A_3(t_0)\\
 t_1^2/2&t_1&1&A_3(t_1)\\
 t_2^2/2&t_2&1&A_3(t_2)\\
 t_3^2/2&t_3&1&A_3(t_3)
 \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}[Uniform observability from four usable $S$ updates]
\label{lem:iss-axis-uco}
For every measurable $\tau(t)$ satisfying
$\underline\tau\le\tau(t)\le\overline\tau$ and any four usable pseudo-updates
obeying Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds},
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 |\det\mat O_S|
 \ge\frac{\Delta_{S,-}^6}{12}
 \exp\!\left(-\frac{T_{S,W}}{\underline\tau}\right)>0.}
 \label{eq:iss-det-lower}
\end{equation}
Hence $[v,p,S,a]$ is uniformly observable on that window.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $f=A_3$.  The divided-difference identity gives
\begin{equation}
 \det\mat O_S
 =-\tfrac12 V(t)A_3[t_0,t_1,t_2,t_3],
 \qquad
 V(t)=\prod_{i<j}(t_j-t_i).
 \label{eq:iss-det-divided-difference}
\end{equation}
Because $A_3'''=E$, the Hermite--Genocchi formula yields
\begin{equation}
 A_3[t_0,t_1,t_2,t_3]
 \ge\frac16\exp\!\left(-\frac{t_3}{\underline\tau}\right)
 \ge\frac16\exp\!\left(-\frac{T_{S,W}}{\underline\tau}\right).
\end{equation}
Equation~\eqref{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds} gives
$V(t)\ge\Delta_{S,-}^6$.  Substitution proves
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-det-lower}.  Compactness of the bounded timing and $\tau$
family converts the determinant and entry bounds into a uniform positive lower
bound on $\sigma_{\min}(\mat O_S)$.
\end{proof}

For the normal time-based scheduler a simple source-level witness is
\begin{equation}
 t_3-t_0\le3(T_{S,\max}+h_{\max}),
 \qquad
 \Delta_{S,-}\ge h_{\min}
 \label{eq:iss-scheduler-gap-witness}
\end{equation}
when no firings are skipped.  The theorem itself is more general and permits
skips whenever Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds} remains true.
The three-axis 12-state translational result follows componentwise.

\subsection{Windowed asynchronous attitude/bias excitation}
\label{sec:iss-windowed-pe}

Order the remaining error coordinates as
\begin{equation}
 \vct\eta
 =\begin{bmatrix}
 \delta\vct\theta^\top&
 \delta\vct b_g^\top&
 \delta\vct b_a^\top
 \end{bmatrix}^{\!\top}\in\mathbb R^9.
 \label{eq:iss-eta-state}
\end{equation}
Let $\mat\Phi_\eta(i,k)$ be their homogeneous transition from $k$ to $i$.  It
contains the exact attitude/gyro-bias transition and either
$e^{-h/\tau_b}\mat I$ or $\mat I$ in the homogeneous accelerometer-bias block;
the latter covers the random-walk/constant-bias limit.

After the translational contribution $\mat R_{wb}\delta\vct a_w$ is placed in
the already observable translational block, the vector-measurement rows acting
on $\vct\eta$ are
\begin{align}
 \mat C_{a,i}
 &=\begin{bmatrix}
 -[\widehat{\vct f}_{\mathrm{cog},b,i}]_\times&0&\mat I
 \end{bmatrix},\\
 \mat C_{m,i}
 &=\begin{bmatrix}
 -[\widehat{\vct m}_{b,i}]_\times&0&0
 \end{bmatrix}.
 \label{eq:iss-eta-rows}
\end{align}
Let $\mathcal A_{k,N}$ and $\mathcal M_{k,N}$ be the accepted accelerometer and
magnetometer update indices in a window.  They need not coincide.  Define
\begin{align}
 \mat W_\eta(k,N)
 &:={}
 \sum_{i\in\mathcal A_{k,N}}
 \mat\Phi_\eta(i,k)^\top
 \mat C_{a,i}^\top\mat R_{a,i}^{-1}\mat C_{a,i}
 \mat\Phi_\eta(i,k)\\
 &\quad+
 \sum_{i\in\mathcal M_{k,N}}
 \mat\Phi_\eta(i,k)^\top
 \mat C_{m,i}^\top\mat R_{m,i}^{-1}\mat C_{m,i}
 \mat\Phi_\eta(i,k).
 \label{eq:iss-eta-gramian}
\end{align}
The full-PE condition is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \alpha_\eta\mat I_9
 \preceq\mat W_\eta(k,N_\eta)
 \preceq\beta_\eta\mat I_9,
 \qquad 0<\alpha_\eta\le\beta_\eta<\infty,}
 \label{eq:iss-eta-pe}
\end{equation}
uniformly in $k$.  This is a finite-window persistent-excitation condition,
not a simultaneous-vector condition.  It allows unequal sensor rates,
individual rejected measurements, short outages, and rotations exceeding
$90^\circ$; all such behavior is admissible if the accumulated information
still satisfies Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta-pe}.

\begin{lemma}[Full 21-state UCO including accelerometer bias]
\label{lem:iss-21-uco-fullpe}
If Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-axis-uco} holds on each translational proof window and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta-pe} holds on a uniformly bounded vector-update window,
then the complete 21-state linearized system is uniformly completely
observable, including for homogeneous accelerometer-bias dynamics
$\delta b_{a,k+1}=\delta b_{a,k}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Order the state as the 12 translational coordinates followed by $\vct\eta$.
Stack the four $S$ observations and all accepted vector observations over a
common window containing the two component windows.  The resulting observation
operator has block-lower-triangular form
\begin{equation}
 \mat O_{21}=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 \mat O_L&0\\
 \mat D&\mat O_\eta
 \end{bmatrix},
 \label{eq:iss-O21-fullpe}
\end{equation}
where $\mat O_L$ is the three-axis translational operator and $\mat D$ contains
the legitimate acceleration-to-accelerometer coupling.  Lemma
\ref{lem:iss-axis-uco} gives a uniform positive singular-value lower bound for
$\mat O_L$, while Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta-pe} gives one for
$\mat O_\eta$.  Both diagonal blocks are therefore uniformly injective and all
blocks are uniformly bounded; hence $\mat O_{21}$ has a uniform positive
minimum singular value.  In particular, once the $S$ history separates the
latent $a_w$ trajectory, the additive accelerometer residual carries an
independent $\mat I\,\delta b_a$ term, so a constant $b_a$ is not a structural
unobservable mode under the augmented PE condition.
\end{proof}

The lemma removes the previous observability obstruction at
$\tau_b=\infty$.  It does not assert that every motion supplies the required
excitation; Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta-pe} is exactly the condition that distinguishes
an informative window from a weakly excited one.

\subsection{Finite-$\tau_b$ detectability fallback}

When Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta-pe} is too strong, define
$\vct\eta_6=(\delta\vct\theta,\delta\vct b_g)$ and the corresponding windowed
Gramian $\mat W_{\eta_6}$ by deleting the accelerometer-bias columns from
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta-rows}.  Assume
\begin{equation}
 \alpha_6\mat I_6\preceq
 \mat W_{\eta_6}(k,N_6)\preceq\beta_6\mat I_6.
 \label{eq:iss-eta6-pe}
\end{equation}
Together with the translational lemma this makes the 18-state subsystem UCO.
For finite $\tau_b$,
\begin{equation}
 \|\Phi_b(k,j)\|
 \le\exp\!\left[-\frac{(k-j)h_{\min}}{\overline\tau_b}\right],
 \label{eq:iss-bias-contraction}
\end{equation}
so any residual unobserved $b_a$ direction is itself UES.  The standard
block-triangular observer construction then gives uniform detectability of the
complete 21-state system.  Thus finite $\tau_b$ remains a valid fallback, but
is no longer an essential assumption when the full augmented PE condition
holds.

\subsection{Process covariance: finite-horizon rather than pointwise excitation}

Let $\mat Q_k^{\rm eff}\succeq0$ denote the exact discrete process covariance,
including the optional prediction-time PSD $a_w$ covariance maintenance.  Only
a uniform upper bound is required pointwise,
\begin{equation}
 0\preceq\mat Q_k^{\rm eff}\preceq q_+\mat I.
 \label{eq:iss-Q-bounds}
\end{equation}
Individual $\mat Q_k^{\rm eff}$ may therefore be singular.  When a classical
uniform-controllability Riccati route is used, require instead a finite-horizon
process Gramian
\begin{equation}
 \mat W_c(k,N_c)
 :=\sum_{i=k}^{k+N_c-1}
 \mat\Phi(k+N_c,i+1)\mat Q_i^{\rm eff}
 \mat\Phi(k+N_c,i+1)^\top
 \succeq\alpha_c\mat I,
 \qquad \alpha_c>0.
 \label{eq:iss-process-gramian}
\end{equation}
This is strictly weaker than $q_-\mat I\preceq\mat Q_k$ at every sample.  In
particular, integrated OU forcing may reach $(v,p,S,a_w)$ only over several
steps, and a process channel may be zero at isolated samples without violating
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-process-gramian}.  The output-error limit with zero process
noise is also compatible with Kalman stability under UCO; see
\cite{Zhang2017_DiscreteKalmanOE}.  For the exponential conclusion below we use
the finite-horizon condition Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-process-gramian}; this avoids
claiming a uniform exponential rate from an unqualified singular-covariance
case.

\subsection{Full-heading Live UES}

\begin{theorem}[Gauge-referenced 21-state Live UES]
\label{thm:iss-21-ues}
Assume the bounded Live family above, Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-axis-uco}, positive
measurement-covariance bounds, the finite-horizon process condition
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-process-gramian}, no hard reset inside the analyzed interval,
and either
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),leftmargin=1.6em]
 \item the full-PE condition Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta-pe}; or
 \item the reduced condition Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-eta6-pe} together with the
       finite-$\tau_b$ fallback Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-bias-tau-bounds}.
\end{enumerate}
Then the homogeneous full-heading 21-state linearized Kalman estimation-error
transition relative to the adopted horizontal gauge is uniformly exponentially
stable: there exist $M\ge1$ and $0<\rho<1$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\Psi_{21}(k,j)\|\le M\rho^{k-j},\qquad k\ge j.}
 \label{eq:iss-21-ues}
\end{equation}
The full-PE route permits the accelerometer-bias random-walk limit
$\tau_b=\infty$; the fallback route does not.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Under route (a), Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-21-uco-fullpe} gives UCO of the complete
21-state system.  Under route (b), the translational and reduced attitude/bias
windows give an 18-state UCO subsystem while finite-$\tau_b$ contraction makes
the remaining augmentation uniformly detectable.  In either case the
finite-horizon process Gramian supplies uniform complete controllability for the
Riccati recursion without requiring pointwise full-rank process covariance.
Together with Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-R-bounds}, standard discrete LTV
Kalman/Riccati stability theory
\cite{Jazwinski1970_Filtering,AndersonMoore1981_DetectabilityStabilizability}
gives uniform covariance regularity and exponential stability of the
homogeneous Kalman error transition.
\end{proof}

A constant residual accelerometer bias with zero driving covariance is already
observable under route (a) by Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-21-uco-fullpe}; however, the
specific UES statement above invokes Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-process-gramian}.  Thus
the theorem directly covers the random-walk limit with positive driving
covariance.  The exactly deterministic zero-process-noise bias limit can use
output-error Kalman results under UCO, but no stronger uniform exponential rate
is asserted here without the corresponding Riccati hypotheses.

\subsection{Gravity-only stability modulo yaw}
\label{sec:iss-nomag-quotient}

Without a magnetic or equivalent heading reference, absolute yaw is a gauge
symmetry.  Let $\mathcal G_\alpha$ denote a rotation of the adopted horizontal
world frame by $\alpha$ about gravity, applied consistently to attitude and all
horizontal world-frame translational states.  The accelerometer model and the
$S=0$ pseudo-measurement are invariant under this action.  Let
$\vct g_k$ be the tangent of this one-dimensional orbit in local error
coordinates and let $\mat P_k^\perp$ project onto a uniformly chosen complement.
Define the quotient error
\begin{equation}
 \vct e_k^\perp=\mat P_k^\perp\vct e_k.
 \label{eq:iss-nomag-error}
\end{equation}

Let $\mat W_o^{\perp}(k,N)$ be the finite-window observability Gramian formed
from accepted accelerometer and $S$ rows and projected onto that complement.
Assume
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \alpha_\perp\mat I
 \preceq\mat W_o^{\perp}(k,N_\perp)
 \preceq\beta_\perp\mat I,
 \qquad \alpha_\perp>0,}
 \label{eq:iss-nomag-pe}
\end{equation}
uniformly in $k$, together with the projected analogue of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-process-gramian}.

\begin{theorem}[No-magnetometer Live UES modulo heading gauge]
\label{thm:iss-nomag-quotient}
Under Lemma~\ref{lem:iss-axis-uco}, Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-nomag-pe}, bounded
covariances and schedule, the projected homogeneous Live error is UES:
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\vct e_k^\perp\|
 \le M_\perp\rho_\perp^{k-j}\|\vct e_j^\perp\|,
 \qquad 0<\rho_\perp<1.}
 \label{eq:iss-nomag-ues}
\end{equation}
No convergence of absolute yaw is claimed.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The measurement and process models descend to the quotient because the
one-dimensional yaw action is a symmetry of the gravity-only outputs.  The
projected Gramian condition gives UCO on the quotient and the projected process
condition gives the corresponding finite-horizon Riccati controllability.
Applying the same discrete LTV Kalman stability argument on that reduced space
gives Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-nomag-ues}.  The removed gauge coordinate is neutral by
construction rather than incorrectly declared observable.
\end{proof}

This theorem is deliberately a quotient result.  It supports stable tilt and
wave-motion estimation without absolute heading, but it is not a full-heading
21-state UES claim.  A numerical regional certificate for no-magnetometer mode
must likewise use a gauge-invariant metric rather than the full-heading metric
of Sec.~\ref{sec:finite-live-capture}.

\subsection{Local nonlinear ISS}

For the full-heading branch, write the nonlinear local error recursion after
prediction, correction, and MEKF reset as
\begin{equation}
 \vct e_{k+1}
 =\mat A_k^{\rm cl}\vct e_k
 +\vct\varphi_k(\vct e_k)
 +\mat G_k\vct d_k,
 \label{eq:iss-nonlinear-recursion}
\end{equation}
where Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues} supplies UES and, on a local chart,
\begin{equation}
 \|\vct\varphi_k(\vct e)\|
 \le c_\varphi\|\vct e\|^2,
 \qquad \|\vct e\|\le r_0.
 \label{eq:iss-quadratic-remainder}
\end{equation}
The disturbance $\vct d_k$ collects bounded model/sensor mismatch, including
thermal-calibration error, pseudo-measurement model error, non-yaw magnetic
model error, and bounded variation of the adopted horizontal gauge.  A constant
$b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}$ is absorbed by the joint attitude/world-vector gauge
trajectory and therefore does not consume the local error radius.

\begin{theorem}[Local ISS of the gauge-referenced 21-state Live dynamics]
\label{thm:iss-21-local}
Under Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues}, Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-quadratic-remainder}, and
$\|\mat G_k\|\le\overline G$, there exist a sufficiently small invariant
neighborhood, $C\ge1$, $0<\lambda<1$, and $\Gamma<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\vct e_k\|
 \le C\lambda^{k-j}\|\vct e_j\|
 +\Gamma\sup_{j\le i<k}\|\vct d_i\|.}
 \label{eq:iss-local-bound}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Variation of constants and Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-21-ues} give
\begin{align}
 \|e_k\|
 &\le M\rho^{k-j}\|e_j\|\\
 &\quad+M\sum_{i=j}^{k-1}\rho^{k-1-i}
 \left(c_\varphi\|e_i\|^2+\overline G\|d_i\|\right).
\end{align}
Choose $r<r_0$ with $Mc_\varphi r/(1-\rho)<1$.  The quadratic convolution is
then absorbed into a slightly slower exponential factor and the bounded-input
convolution has finite geometric gain.  Shrinking the admissible initial and
input bounds so the trajectory remains in the selected chart closes the usual
local invariant-set argument and yields Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-local-bound}.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Stochastic qualification}
Gaussian process and sensor noise have no finite almost-sure infinite-horizon
supremum, so Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-local-bound} is a deterministic bounded-input
statement.  The regional stochastic theorem of
Sec.~\ref{sec:capture-stochastic} treats mean-square and finite-horizon
probability statements separately.

\paragraph{Scope}
The full-heading UES/ISS proof covers asynchronous accepted vector updates,
short rejection/outage intervals consistent with the PE window, nonconsecutive
$S$ updates satisfying Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-S-gap-bounds}, rotations not restricted
by a $\pi/2$ small-turn condition, singular one-step process covariances
satisfying the finite-horizon process condition, and either augmented
accelerometer-bias PE or the finite-$\tau_b$ fallback.  Startup and hard reset
maps remain outside this Live theorem.  The gravity-only branch is stable only
modulo yaw and therefore carries no absolute-heading convergence claim.
